{"title":"2004 CEMC Fryer Problems/Problem 4b","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_4\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2004 CEMC Fryer Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=T-1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Triangular numbers can be calculated by counting the dots in the following triangular shapes:\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>An image is supposed to go here.  You can help us out by creating one and <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2004_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4b&amp;action=edit\">editing it in</a></span>.  Thanks.</i>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The first triangular number is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the second is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the third is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, the fourth is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />th triangular\nnumber equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/3/1e3c1b2843479998156575a3d1ecf4712b69195e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + (n - 1) + n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"229\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4b&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Prove that the sum of any three consecutive triangular numbers is always <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> more than three times the middle of these three triangular numbers.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4b&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /><sup>th</sup> triangular number is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/3/1e3c1b2843479998156575a3d1ecf4712b69195e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + (n - 1) + n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"229\" height=\"20\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/5/c55fa61f6f77bd5e9dc733cb36e4c7372130dfb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n(n + 1)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"69\" height=\"38\" /> for all natural <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>Lwt <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> be the smallest number for the triangular number, meaning that we have the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /><sup>th</sup> triangular number, the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/8/e18453e31b8b9f4f7722777b72a0eff030a17d1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"40\" height=\"14\" /><sup>th</sup> triangular number, and the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/7/317f612a109703b31a6021ae4d951a52b05ee0a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k + 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"41\" height=\"14\" /><sup>th</sup> triangular number. The triangular numbers are then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/3/9f3860c4f1dae43156258b03492a228fc609d9d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{k(k + 1)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"68\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/0/820f5856381583b11f20f2109fa91c840343eae7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(k + 1)(k + 2)}{2} = \\frac{k^{2} + 3k + 2}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"235\" height=\"41\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/d/9ed5b9db871636eb294c4a5715cb17a6a30515a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(k + 2)(k + 3)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"117\" height=\"40\" />. Their sum would be:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/b/9ab8595137316232e0e3acf7667fd6ccc5c18655.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{k(k + 1) + (k + 1)(k + 2) + (k + 2)(k + 3)}{2} = \\frac{k^{2} + k + k^{2} + 3k + 2 + k^{2} + 5k + 6}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"640\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/7/2275d9a78a7ba6d683d706db5076fee68f4ba091.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$=\\frac{3k^{2} + 9k + 6 + 2}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"156\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/e/21ed939889b2260a18794e476a0028acfeb6bc05.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$=\\frac{3(k^{2} + 9k + 6)}{2} + \\frac{2}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"174\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/9/62950230f78799cdb963814d28e569f20c21566b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$=3 \\times \\frac{k^{2} + 3k + 2}{2} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"178\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>We can realize that this is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/8/e18453e31b8b9f4f7722777b72a0eff030a17d1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"40\" height=\"14\" /><sup>th</sup> triangular number (the middle triangular number) + <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, the sum of any three consecutive triangular numbers is always <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> more than three times the middle of these three triangular numbers.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017203814\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.018 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.034 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 96/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1173/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 5/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4858/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   13.511      1 2004_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_4\n100.00%   13.511      1 -total\n 38.52%    5.204      1 Template:Image_needed\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:37018:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017203814 and revision id 285988. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}